Agent Prayers


Introduction

One of the characteristic features of Ouroborus is that agents living on the 
cellular automaton grid can be thought of as a number of things, depending on 
one's focus. Thus agents are:

1) a string containing a sequence of letter groupings codifying genetic 
information, i.e. a genome,
2) a script in Pyrex with some executable code written from the genome. Python 
imports this into the runtime envirnoment, i.e. a module,
3) a dictionary-type object, with various attributes such as name, genome, 
module, grid address and so on, i.e. a bookentry in a list which is an attribute
of GOD.Organizer, or,
4) a birdcage agent object which is called by GOD.Organizer through the
bookentry.

It is of course naturally simple for GOD.Organizer to send information to the 
agents, via the respective bookentries which are callable subobjects of a simple
list attribute. GOD.Organizer can retrieve information from the agents with the 
same ease, when this is part of the silicon demiurge's execution loop. However 
some more elaborate means of data transmission is required for the agents 
themselves to send information to GOD.Organizer, so to speak out of their own 
volition. It is exactly for this purpose that agent prayers were introduced.


Technical Details

The GOD module consists of three main objects, Generator, Organizer and 
Destroyer. The Generator is in charge of initialising everything and the 
Destroyer's role is left as an exercise to the reader. (A very trivial one.) It 
is the Organizer which concerns us here, as it manages a list of bookentries, 
called book (of life) through which it interprets the agent's messages to the 
world around them. 

An agent's bookentry is a complex data type which includes the following 
information:

a) name - a sequential name for each agent, 
b) modulename - a name for the strain, this is the same as 'name' if individual 
compilation is selected,
c) module - the pyrex module object imported into the python runtime 
environment,
d) agent - the corresponding birdcage agent object,
e) automaton - the birdcage automaton object which is the agent's environment, 
and, 
f) fatum - a python dictionary which contains miscellaneous information.

Among this 'miscellaneous information' there is an all-important datum, under 
the key 'prayer'. The associated value is a string which contains the agent's 
intention as it projects out to the world. As this is written in GOD.Organizer's
book, this superior entity acknowledges the agents' prayers and grants them by 
calling the corresponding methods. This, in turn, normally involves calling the 
agent's own methods (decoded from its genome and written in its pyrex module), 
which will return a string to be written in the bookentry's fatum as the next 
prayer, and so forth until the end of the execution cycle.

All the prayers have corresponding methods in the Organizer. Their names are 
prefixed by the string "grantPrayer" followed by the prayer name. These methods 
are called through introspection (using the getattr function) by the 
readBookOfLife method of the Organizer. It is important to notice that the 
prayer method in question is being called and returned simultaneously by the 
readBookofLife method's return statement.

So far, the following prayers have been implemented:

1) CreateMe
This prayer should be set when an agent is first created, i.e. at the beginning 
of the simulation (this prayer's equivalent once the simulation is running is
GrantChild). It populates the new agent's bookentry. In doing that it sets its
prayer to BeBirthed. 'Phenotypical' characteristics are also set here 
(currently, only the voice coloration and pitch range).

2) BeBirthed
This method takes the newly created agent and makes it live, that is, it
registers the agent with the relevant automaton instance, as well as calling
any other method needed to initialise it. Last, but not least, it sets the 
prayer to Live.

3) Live
This is the default prayer, it executes the main loop of the agent, so to speak.
(The agents don't actually have a main loop, but more on this later.) Then, in 
case they changed during the former's execution, it updates the 'prana' and 
'address' values in the agent's bookentry.fatum dictionary. It also checks if 
the music is on and if the agent has eaten, in which case it sets the 
fatum["voice"] object's 'ate' attribute to '1', so that the appropiate sound 
gets 'rendered'.

4) GrantChild
This prayer is a request for reproduction. It resets the parent's prayer to 
Live. (NOTE: Oddly enough, it sets the childs prayer to BeBirthed, thus 
contradicting this text, as well as the systems architechture as this author, 
Jergas, understands it. MEA CULPA: Methinks this is my fault, as I remeber 
having hacked this aspect of the prayer system before properly understanding 
it.) 'Phenotypical' characteristics are also set here (currently,
only the voice coloration and pitch range).

5) KillMe
This prayer is a destructor method, so to speak, it should contain everything 
needed to properly erase (or kill) an agent. For obvious reasons, this is the
only prayer, apart from Live, that doesn't need to reset the calling agent's 
prayer.

The Live prayer deserves special notice because of its singular method of 
execution. The method GOD.Organizer.grantPrayerLive calls the agentLive method 
of the agent's own BookEntry. The first thing the latter method does is call the
'live' method of the module object, which is referenced by the bookentry's 
'module' attribute; since this is all happening in the agent's BookEntry, 
the module can be accessed through the self parameter. Of course, this means 
that any valid genome, when compiled, must implement the live function. This 
function must accept exactly one parameter, a birdcage agent object, and must be
passed the agent itself when called, so that the agent may know things about 
itself as it carries on the business of living.